<section id="A6.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.5 </span>Limitations</h3>

<div id="A6.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These profiling results measure systems cost, not sample quality.
They use synthetic states and random targets to isolate architectural scaling.
The token baseline is the most direct continuous token-boundary comparison; embedding-space or discrete-token diffusion variants may differ in input-state cost, loss implementation, and kernel efficiency.
However, methods that train through vocabulary-wide logits, masked-token scores, or transition scores retain an <math id="A6.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(BTV)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(BTV)</annotation></semantics></math> output/loss boundary.
Bitstream Diffusion removes this boundary while preserving the semantic Transformer sequence length <math id="A6.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Qualitative evaluation</h2>

